C7  - 112191
DO  - 10.1016/j.rineng.2026.112191
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046003659?origin=resultslist
AB  - Accurate biomechanical characterization of the cornea is essential for early detection and progression assessment of keratoconus, particularly in subclinical stages where conventional metrics lack sensitivity. This study presents a novel computational framework integrating patient-specific finite element modeling with microstructural tissue analysis to establish elastic strain energy density (ESED) as a mechanistically grounded biomarker of disease severity. An anisotropic hyperelastic Gasser–Holzapfel–Ogden model incorporating collagen fiber organization and proteoglycan cross-linking was applied to 40 corneas spanning different severity stages under controlled intraocular pressure conditions. The results revealed a nonlinear increase in ESED with disease progression, accompanied by a loss of symmetry in the posterior central cornea associated with collagen disorganization. A mechanistic interpretation linking macroscopic energy accumulation to proteoglycan-level interactions is proposed, highlighting a progressive reduction in the tissue’s capacity to store and redistribute mechanical energy. The ESED-based classification captures the continuous nature of disease progression and overcomes limitations of discrete diagnostic metrics. This framework provides a physically interpretable and patient-specific approach for improved early detection, severity stratification, and progression monitoring of keratoconus. © 2026 The Authors.
